Reason:
The `match_char` macro evaluates its character parameter multiple times inside its internal do-while loop. When the parameter is an expression with side effects like `*str++`, `str` is incremented each time the macro evaluates the parameter, causing the string pointer to advance more than once per outer loop iteration. This skips input characters and leads to out-of-bounds reads when the pointer advances past the buffer boundary.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
while (*str) {
